WooCommerce 11.3 MCP Adaptor updates Advisories – The WooCommerce Developer Blog WooCommerce 11.3 MCP Adaptor updates Darren Ethier • Oct 21, 2026 Advisories , Engineering Starting with WooCommerce 11.3, WooCommerce core product and order abilities will be enabled by default, and will require the WordPress MCP Adapter plugin active on your store to establish an MCP connection. This update will remove the /wp-json/woocommerce/mcp endpoint and legacy abilities that have been deprecated since WooCommerce 10.9 . If your MCP client connects to your store with the deprecated endpoint and a WooCommerce REST API key, you will need to update that connection to use the WordPress MCP Adapter plugin and a WordPress application password before testing or upgrading to 11.3. Enabling MCP in WooCommerce 11.3 MCP Adapter 0.7.0 is the first release published as a WordPress.org plugin, and it deprecates bundling the adapter as a Composer library . With one shared adapter per site, a separate WooCommerce endpoint with its own authentication and its own set of REST-shaped tools only added configuration and confusion. The purpose-built WooCommerce abilities replace the REST-derived ones, and the adapter’s default server already exposes them, along with abilities registered by other plugins on your site. We’re also working on bringing more abilities to Woo core and our extensions to round out the type of actions and tools available for humans and agents working with WooCommerce. What’s removed The /wp-json/woocommerce/mcp endpoint and its WooCommerce REST API key authentication (the X-MCP-API-Key header). Requests to it now return HTTP 410 with a JSON-RPC error that explains how to connect through the MCP Adapter. The WooCommerce MCP feature ( mcp_integration ) under WooCommerce > Settings > Advanced > Features . FeaturesUtil::feature_is_enabled( 'mcp_integration' ) now always returns false . The REST-derived abilities that only the deprecated endpoint used: woocommerce/products-list , woocommerce/products-get , woocommerce/products-create , woocommerce/products-update , woocommerce/products-delete , woocommerce/orders-list , woocommerce/orders-get , woocommerce/orders-create , and woocommerce/orders-update . Filters that only applied to the deprecated endpoint: woocommerce_mcp_include_ability , woocommerce_mcp_allow_insecure_transport , and woocommerce_check_rest_ability_permissions_for_method . Callbacks attached to them no longer run. The expose_in_deprecated_woocommerce_mcp ability metadata is no longer read. Bundled packages : wordpress/mcp-adapter and wordpress/abilities-api . The Abilities API is part of WordPress core as of WordPress 7.0, WooCommerce’s minimum version. The MCP Adapter project has deprecated the package in favor of the canonical plugin (which is built from the same repo). How can developers tell if they are affected? You are affected if any of these apply: An MCP client connects to /wp-json/woocommerce/mcp with a WooCommerce REST API key. Your extension registers abilities that it expected to appear in the WooCommerce MCP endpoint, through the woocommerce/ namespace, expose_in_deprecated_woocommerce_mcp , or the woocommerce_mcp_include_ability filter. Your code hooks into woocommerce_mcp_include_ability , woocommerce_mcp_allow_insecure_transport , or woocommerce_check_rest_ability_permissions_for_method . Your code uses WP\MCP\* classes and relies on WooCommerce to load them. Your code calls MCPAdapterProvider , WooCommerceRestTransport , AbilitiesRestBridge , or RestAbilityFactory , or one of the removed REST-derived ability IDs listed above. What action do developers need to take if affected? Merchants and MCP client users If you connected an AI assistant to /wp-json/woocommerce/mcp : Install and activate the MCP Adapter plugin , for example with wp plugin install mcp-adapter --activate . Create a WordPress application password for a user with only the capabilities the client needs. Point your MCP client at /wp-json/mcp/mcp-adapter-default-server with that username and application password, instead of the WooCommerce endpoint and REST API key. The WooCommerce MCP documentation has client configuration examples. Stores that had the WooCommerce MCP feature enabled see an admin notice after updating. It links to the documentation and, when no MCP adapter is loaded, to Install MCP Adapter (which opens the official plugin’s details) or Activate MCP Adapter if the plugin is already installed. The notice goes away for every admin once someone dismisses it or activates the plugin from it. You can revoke the WooCommerce REST API keys you created for the old endpoint. Extension developers Register abilities through the WordPress Abilities API and add mcp metadata ( 'mcp' => array( 'public' => true, 'type' => 'tool' ) ) so the MCP Adapter exposes them. Custom abilities that appeared in the removed endpoint don’t carry over: before 10.9 it included abilities in the woocommerce/ namespace, from 10.9 those with expose_in_deprecated_woocommerce_mcp set to true , and the woocommerce_mcp_include_ability filter could change either. None of these affect the MCP Adapter’s default server, which only exposes abilities whose meta.mcp.public is true (falling back to the top-level meta.public ). Don’t rely on WooCommerce for MCP Adapter classes. If your extension uses WP\MCP\* classes, check for them at runtime (see below), or add Requires Plugins: mcp-adapter if your extension can’t work without MCP. Removed internal classes don’t fatal. MCPAdapterProvider , WooCommerceRestTransport , AbilitiesRestBridge , and RestAbilityFactory still exist, but their methods do nothing except trigger a “called incorrectly” notice with migration guidance. Remove any calls to them. WooCommerceRestTransport no longer extends the MCP adapter’s HttpTransport , so code that type-hints or instanceof -checks HttpTransport won’t accept it, and passing it to the adapter’s create_server() fails. Use the adapter’s own HttpTransport with application passwords, or your own transport. if ( class_exists( 'WP\MCP\Core\McpAdapter' ) ) {
    // Register your MCP server or tools.
} if ( class_exists( 'WP\MCP\Core\McpAdapter' ) ) {
    // Register your MCP server or tools.
